   "flags": {},
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "widgets_values": [
    "## sv3d-turnaround.json\n\nOne image -> a consistent multi-view turnaround (no mesh needed)\n\n**Does:** SV3D_p takes a single image and renders the same object from fixed azimuths. One batch, four views at 0/90/180/270, in ~15 s on the 3090 at ~7 GB VRAM (E9).\n**Why:** `hy3d-multiview` needs front/left/right/back, and nothing in the repo made them - the flow presumed an asset we never had. This is that asset, and it needs no mesh, so it also serves cases where only views are wanted (a sprite that must read from several angles, a reference sheet).\n**The knob that matters:** `azimuth_points_string` / `elevation_points_string` - the point index is the frame, so `0:(0.0), 1:(90.0), 2:(180.0), 3:(270.0)` is four evenly spaced views and `batch_size` must equal the last index + 1. Raise `batch_size` to 21 with the default 0/180/360 points for a smooth orbit instead of four stops. `VideoLinearCFGGuidance` is required (SV3D is an image-to-video model), and `elevation` stays at 0 for a level turnaround.\n**Licence:** the SV3D weights are Stability **non-commercial** (commercial use needs a Stability Membership), so anything shipped from this flow inherits that - same posture as Kontext and 4x-UltraSharp in the 2D scenario. The weights live in `models/checkpoints/sv3d_p.safetensors` and are gated on Hugging Face (`stabilityai/sv3d`).\n**See:** `3d/EXPERIMENTS.md` E9, `3d/README.md`.\n\n---\n*How to run it: story **S12** in `HOWTO.md`  ·  Why it is this way: **E9** in the scenario's `EXPERIMENTS` logs.*"
   ],
   "properties": {},
   "order": 0
  }
